:\n        dummy = ListNode()\n        tail = dummy\n        carry = 0\n        while l1 or l2 or carry:\n            total = carry\n            if l1:\n                total += l1.val\n                l1 = l1.next\n            if l2:\n                total += l2.val\n                l2 = l2.next\n            carry, digit = divmod(total, 10)\n            tail.next = ListNode(digit)\n            tail = tail.next\n        return dummy.next\n","approach":"**Key insight: the reversed storage order makes this grade-school addition.** Start at the least significant digit, add, keep the carry, move on. No conversion to integers is needed (and converting would fail for numbers longer than a machine word).\n\n**Algorithm.** Keep a dummy head, a tail pointer and `carry = 0`. Loop while either list has nodes *or* the carry is non-zero. Sum the available digits plus the carry, append `total % 10`, set `carry = total // 10`. Return `dummy.next`.\n\n**Why it works.** Each iteration produces exactly one output digit in the correct position and forwards the carry, which is the definition of positional addition. Including `carry` in the loop condition emits the extra leading digit when the sum is longer than both inputs.\n\n**Common mistakes.** Stopping when the lists end and dropping a final carry (`[5] + [5]` must give `[0,1]`). Writing separate loops for the common prefix and for each leftover list, which triples the chance of a bug. Converting to Python ints, which works here but misses the point of the question.\n\n**Interview follow-ups.** (1) Add Two Numbers II: digits stored most-significant first; reverse the lists, or use two stacks, or recurse after padding to equal length. (2) Multiply two numbers stored as lists. (3) Subtract (handle sign and borrow), or add numbers in base b.","complexity":{"time":"O(max(n, m))","space":"O(max(n, m)) for the output"},"similar":["add-two-numbers-ii","add-binary","multiply-strings"],"followups":["Most-significant-digit-first variant.","Multiplication."],"number":54,"starter":"class Solution:\n    def addTwoNumbers(self, l1: Optional[ListNode], l2: Optional[ListNode]) -> Optional[ListNode]:\n        pass\n"}